      "content": "Certainly, let's delve into the specifics of why iron and manganese behave in such a seemingly abrupt manner in the soil profile. The suddenness in their transition from available to unavailable can be attributed to several factors related to their chemistry and interactions with the soil matrix.\n\nFirstly, the solubility of iron and manganese is highly pH-dependent. At lower pH levels, these metals are more soluble and exist predominantly in the form of Fe²⁺ and Mn²⁺ ions. As the pH increases, the solubility of these ions decreases rapidly due to the formation of insoluble hydroxides, Fe(OH)2 and Mn(OH)2. This is a relatively sharp transition because the solubility of these ions is governed by the solubility product constants (Ksp) of their respective hydroxides, which are quite low. This means that even small increases in pH can lead to a significant decrease in the concentration of free Fe²⁺ and Mn²⁺ ions in the soil solution.\n\nSecondly, the soil matrix itself can influence the availability of these nutrients. Iron and manganese have a strong affinity for binding to organic matter and clay particles. As the pH increases, the charge on these soil particles can change",
